So that's Type I.

11:01.360 --> 11:04.300
But now, later layer in the C-set, the governing relations.

11:05.440 --> 11:08.640
Let's say the reactor is currently running dangerously over pressure.

11:09.180 --> 11:11.440
The safety protocols hard-code a relation.

11:12.300 --> 11:16.420
If it's over pressure, the only valid action is to vent the system.

11:16.640 --> 11:17.880
This is where we see the divergence.

11:18.880 --> 11:22.400
Restarting the process is perfectly physically feasible for the technician.

11:22.580 --> 11:23.460
They can reach the button.

11:23.600 --> 11:24.580
It sits in the F-set.

11:24.580 --> 11:27.860
But it is strictly invalid under the governing relation.

11:28.000 --> 11:28.060
Right.

11:28.140 --> 11:29.380
It violates the safety rules.

11:29.560 --> 11:29.680
Right.

11:29.900 --> 11:34.680
So if the technician attempts to restart it, it triggers a Type II failure, a relational failure.

11:35.140 --> 11:36.500
Feasible but invalid.

11:36.780 --> 11:41.560
And this highlights exactly why your standard, like, Python try-except-block

11:41.560 --> 11:45.840
or returning a generic action-rejected message is entirely inadequate.

