Now, elevated could mean a genuine overpressure situation, which means they must vent to satisfy the C layer.

13:21.440 --> 13:21.680
Right.

13:21.680 --> 13:27.500
Or elevated could just be a minor thermal false alarm where venting would just waste a ton of resources.

13:27.500 --> 13:32.200
This is what Flixion formally introduces as the diagnostic identifiability problem.

13:32.900 --> 13:41.720
You have a true state of the world that demands a specific intervention, but your sensor topology maps two totally different underlying realities to the exact same observation.

13:41.860 --> 13:43.760
Oh, D of by one equals D of by two.

13:43.760 --> 13:44.320
Exactly.

13:44.660 --> 13:51.500
When that happens, no downstream AI, no brilliant algorithmic planning engine, can magically deduce the correct action.

13:51.980 --> 13:55.300
The flaw is in the partition design, not the decision algorithm.

13:55.860 --> 14:01.620
If your sensor lumps two states together that require different treatments, the system is fundamentally incapable of establishing validity.

14:02.080 --> 14:03.660
It's a type 3 failure.

14:04.040 --> 14:08.580
So, as a systems architect, my immediate instinct here is just brute force.

