Why not?

14:30.640 --> 14:36.540
Because high-dimensional data actually introduces noise, overfitting, and catastrophic latency.

14:36.540 --> 14:40.560
The goal Fliction introduces is equivalence class engineering.

14:40.840 --> 14:42.220
Equivalence class engineering, okay.

14:42.360 --> 14:44.280
You don't need a high-def 4K camera.

14:44.720 --> 14:48.660
You just need a partition that specifically separates the faults needing different treatments.

14:49.360 --> 14:57.580
If state A and state B both require the reactor to keep running normally, your sensor should mathematically lump them together into the same equivalence class.

14:57.660 --> 15:00.420
Because distinguishing between them is computationally wasteful.

15:00.740 --> 15:01.340
Exactly.

15:01.740 --> 15:04.720
It epistemically irrelevant to the governing rules.

15:04.720 --> 15:08.340
More data does not equal more relevant distinctions.

15:08.980 --> 15:15.300
You engineer your sensors so their equivalence classes map one-to-one with the valid actions in your C-set.

15:15.680 --> 15:16.480
That is profound.

15:17.060 --> 15:19.360
You only partition what needs different treatment.

15:19.540 --> 15:19.680
Right.

15:19.900 --> 15:22.780
But getting the right data at the right time is hard enough.

15:23.060 --> 15:26.440
What happens when a single nose literally cannot achieve that partition?

15:26.720 --> 15:26.940
Right.

15:27.040 --> 15:29.020
This brings in GAP2, distributed knowledge.

