And if you ask technician B, their case set is also empty.

16:00.480 --> 16:02.600
Because noisy could just be a loose bracket rattling.

16:03.000 --> 16:03.740
Neither has proof.

16:03.740 --> 16:07.680
So if I just take the union of their localized conclusions, it's still empty.

16:07.900 --> 16:08.820
The system halts.

16:09.300 --> 16:09.660
Exactly.

16:09.820 --> 16:12.100
You cannot just pool their final conclusions.

16:12.760 --> 16:19.100
But if you look at their composed evaluation positions, the intersection of their raw sensory

16:19.100 --> 16:25.100
data, the combined data point of elevated and noisy completely rules out the false alarms.

16:25.320 --> 16:25.800
Oh, wow.

16:26.160 --> 16:30.460
Because the false thermal alarm doesn't cause noise and the loose bracket doesn't cause pressure.

16:30.700 --> 16:31.200
Precisely.

16:31.200 --> 16:34.800
The composed observation certifies the venting action.

16:35.280 --> 16:36.640
You cannot just pool conclusions.

16:36.840 --> 16:40.200
You must compose the evaluation positions before you evaluate the rules.

16:40.360 --> 16:45.080
Which means in a distributed network, you have to stream the raw partition data to a central

16:45.080 --> 16:47.540
layer, intersect it, and then check authorization.

16:47.860 --> 16:48.220
Exactly.

16:48.460 --> 16:48.660
Okay.

16:48.740 --> 16:50.180
So that solves the epistemic gap.

16:50.260 --> 16:53.040
We compose the state, establish the case set, and act.

16:53.420 --> 16:56.860
But everything we've talked about so far assumes a static environment, right?

16:57.020 --> 16:57.260
Right.

16:57.340 --> 16:58.540
A static state machine.

16:58.660 --> 17:00.360
But reality isn't a static.

17:00.360 --> 17:04.640
What happens when the simple act of making a decision fundamentally changes the rules

17:04.640 --> 17:05.300
for the next one?

17:05.940 --> 17:07.740
This brings us to part six of the text.

17:07.840 --> 17:11.140
The march of time, histories, and non-monotonicity.

17:11.580 --> 17:15.500
And this is where traditional state machine architectures completely break down.

17:15.700 --> 17:15.840
Yeah.

17:15.840 --> 17:20.940
We all have this intuitive assumption that as time passes and we collect more records in

17:20.940 --> 17:23.920
our database, our epistemic certainty grows.

17:24.320 --> 17:24.600
Right.

17:24.980 --> 17:25.120
Right.

17:25.180 --> 17:30.160
It append-only law gives me more context, so I should have a wider array of valid actions

17:30.160 --> 17:30.660
I can take.

17:31.180 --> 17:33.480
More history means more justified action.

17:33.480 --> 17:37.000
But fliction proves this is a structural fallacy.

17:37.000 --> 17:39.580
The theorem of non-monotonic justification.

17:40.580 --> 17:45.660
The paper formally proves that more history does not entail more currently justified action.

17:46.060 --> 17:46.160
Okay.

17:46.260 --> 17:50.120
We need to trace this sequence because this destroys how we normally build state machines.

17:50.640 --> 17:52.060
Let's go back to the technician.

17:52.300 --> 17:52.500
Okay.

17:52.500 --> 17:54.980
The system is at time zero, H zero.

17:55.780 --> 17:59.940
The composite observer has established that pressure is elevated and there is noise.

18:00.460 --> 18:02.220
They are fully certified to vent the machine.

18:02.340 --> 18:02.500
Right.

18:02.800 --> 18:08.160
But critically, at time zero, before they vent, the rules also state they are perfectly authorized

18:08.160 --> 18:09.440
to just restart the machine.

18:09.560 --> 18:10.480
It is in the C set.

18:10.520 --> 18:10.660
Right.

18:10.720 --> 18:11.120
It's valid.

18:11.360 --> 18:12.280
But they choose to vent.

18:12.580 --> 18:16.460
So if the venting action fires, the state transitions from H zero to H one.

18:16.780 --> 18:18.220
And this is the pivot.

18:18.940 --> 18:22.160
The physical act of venting doesn't just change the physical pressure.

18:22.160 --> 18:24.020
It triggers a new historical rule.

18:24.020 --> 18:31.020
The safety protocol dictates a post vent system must be recalibrated before restarting.

18:31.220 --> 18:36.420
Oh, so you're saying a perfect mathematically valid decision at 2.0 p.m.

18:36.420 --> 18:40.860
is exactly what makes that same decision illegal at 2.01 p.m.

18:40.980 --> 18:41.520
Exactly.

18:42.280 --> 18:43.720
Restarting is suddenly invalid.

18:43.920 --> 18:45.240
It drops out of the C set.

18:45.760 --> 18:49.380
The system's own action changed the future decision problem.

18:50.000 --> 18:52.320
Decision systems are dynamical systems.

18:52.520 --> 18:52.860
Wow.

18:52.860 --> 18:58.660
If you model a system as a series of isolated moments, without recognizing that its output

18:58.660 --> 19:02.960
reshakes the next instance inputs, you will build a system that inevitably breaks its own

19:02.960 --> 19:03.320
rules.

19:03.500 --> 19:05.020
That is so true for our own lives, too.

19:05.520 --> 19:10.000
Taking action closes some doors while opening others, but structurally, it reshapes the governing

19:10.000 --> 19:11.960
relation of what we are allowed to do next.

19:12.060 --> 19:12.400
Yes.

19:12.540 --> 19:14.160
But this introduces a huge complication.

19:14.160 --> 19:18.120
If the rules change based on history and identity, then merely looking at the physical

19:18.120 --> 19:19.980
outcome of an action is entirely inadequate.

19:19.980 --> 19:21.840
We have to look at relational identity.

19:22.060 --> 19:25.140
Which is the core focus of Chapter 20, beyond physical state.

19:25.440 --> 19:25.620
Right.

19:25.680 --> 19:27.340
So Flixing introduces this notation.

19:27.640 --> 19:32.140
T of H, U equals T of H, V. But R of H, U does not equal R, J of H, V.

19:33.260 --> 19:37.260
Why doesn't the author care just about what an action does to the physical world?

19:37.860 --> 19:43.060
Because two actions can induce the exact same physical state transition in the world, but

19:43.060 --> 19:45.360
have completely different relational validity.

19:45.600 --> 19:47.940
Isn't that just a long way of saying context matters?

19:47.940 --> 19:49.920
It's far more rigorous than that.

19:50.200 --> 19:51.820
Think about a distributed database.

19:52.520 --> 19:58.100
Actor I is a citizen, like a senior database admin, wanting a routine script to delete a

19:58.100 --> 19:59.020
deprecated file.

19:59.280 --> 19:59.560
Okay.

19:59.840 --> 20:05.940
Actor J is a malicious hacker who bypasses the firewall and executes an exploit to delete

20:05.940 --> 20:07.120
that exact same file.

20:07.560 --> 20:12.940
If you only measure the physical state transition, what Flixing calls T, the result is identical.

20:13.140 --> 20:14.080
The file is gone.

20:14.180 --> 20:14.400
Right.

20:14.540 --> 20:15.740
The hard drive sectors are wiped.

20:15.740 --> 20:18.960
But the relational status Rye versus RJ is totally opposite.

20:19.240 --> 20:20.820
One was authorized, one was a breach.

20:21.200 --> 20:23.060
Some rules are irreducibly relational.

20:23.360 --> 20:26.040
They exist only between specific actors and histories.

20:26.380 --> 20:31.240
So if your observability platform only monitors physical state like CPU load or table size,

20:31.580 --> 20:32.500
it won't see the hack.

20:32.840 --> 20:33.400
Precisely.

20:33.900 --> 20:37.940
And this is why Flixing introduces evidence objects, denoted as E.

20:38.320 --> 20:40.420
A decision shouldn't just assert a fact.

20:40.620 --> 20:43.800
It shouldn't just return a Boolean, true for authorized.

20:43.800 --> 20:44.360
Right.

20:44.580 --> 20:50.320
It needs a tangible evidence object, a cryptographic signature, provenance, a timestamp.

20:50.580 --> 20:57.720
When the K-layer establishes validity, it binds the action to this evidence object so it can be audited later.

20:57.940 --> 21:02.100
That sounds like the blueprint for a bulletproof legal or institutional system.

21:02.160 --> 21:02.960
It really is.

